=== but is SEEING the partner accurate enough? ===
  displacement budget: 0.161 m (median indoor object, IoU@0.3)

   object range from partner  indoor heading budget  outdoor (car) budget
                     4.0 m                 2.31 deg              17.62 deg
                     8.0 m                 1.15 deg               8.81 deg
                    15.0 m                 0.62 deg               4.70 deg
                    22.4 m                 0.41 deg               3.15 deg

  So even with the partner's POSITION known exactly, its HEADING has
  to be right to about 1 degree for mid-range objects and 0.4 degrees
  at the edge of its range. A car outdoors gets 8.8 and 3.1 degrees.
  Median inter-robot distance here is 8.04 m, so a
  robot is a small, roughly symmetric blob at that range - recovering
  its yaw to a degree from one LiDAR view is not a solved problem.

  This is the honest answer to 'why not just look at the other robot':
  seeing it gives you position, and position is not what breaks us.
